     license + exception text intact) alongside any redistribution
     of your application that includes the SDK.

  3. Your application code is in separate files from the SDK files
     — i.e. your code calls INTO the SDK, rather than being woven
     into it.

This exception applies to BOTH source and binary forms of the
Combined Work, including container images that bundle the SDK with
proprietary application code.


WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER
------------------------

This exception does NOT extend to:

  - The rest of the Yorik core (backend/main.py, frontend/, etc.).
    Code that imports from, modifies, or links against those files
    is governed by the unmodified AGPL-3.0-or-later.

  - Your distribution of a modified Yorik core itself. The AGPL's
    network-use clause still applies to the core platform.

  - Third-party libraries the SDK happens to import (httpx, pydantic,
    etc.). Those are governed by their own licenses.

If your application talks to Yorik only via HTTP (an iframe or a
separate process making API calls), then the AGPL's combined-work
clause arguably never engages in the first place — but this exception
gives explicit permission for the linking case too, removing all
ambiguity for app authors.


WHY
---

Yorik wants a healthy ecosystem of third-party apps — including
commercial ones from developers who want to charge money to support
their work. Without this exception, the AGPL's copyleft would force
every app built on the SDK to also be AGPL, which would block
proprietary commercial apps entirely. By carving out the SDK as a
permissive linking boundary, we preserve the AGPL's protection of
the core platform (anyone distributing a modified Yorik must share
their changes) while letting the app ecosystem be a mix of free,
open-source, freemium, and commercial offerings.

This is analogous to the GCC Runtime Library Exception, the
Classpath Exception used by OpenJDK, and the linking exceptions
used by GNU Bison and others — all longstanding patterns for
keeping copyleft platforms commercially extensible.


